details.contract:
app/bin/codegraph.ts keeps its shebang, its lazy `const { main } = await import("../src/index")` import and its `process.exit(await main(requested, argv))` tail. Replace the four-name MODES allow-list with `const RESERVED = ["mcp", "worker", "index"] as const` and make the entry accept either a reserved verb or a tool name:

const argv = process.argv.slice(2);
const requested = argv[0] ?? "mcp";

Help: when `requested` is `--help`, `-h` or `help`, print USAGE to stdout and exit 0 without importing the kernel. When `requested` is a reserved verb and argv contains `--help` or `-h`, print that verb's one-line usage (`cgraph index [--mode full|fast] [--force] [--repo-root P] [--data-dir P]`, with the mcp/worker equivalents) and exit 0 — never let `--help` fall through to a verb that would actually run an index.

Otherwise call main(requested, argv) and, when it returns 2 (the usage exit code), print USAGE to stderr and exit 2. Every other code is passed through unchanged. The bin file must not import #shared/* or the tool registry; the usage banner is a literal naming the three verbs, the seven tools (noting that `index` is both a verb and a tool, with identical arguments), the global flags `--repo-root`, `--data-dir`, `--json`, and two example lines: `cgraph trace 'handleRequest' --depth 1` and `cgraph mcp --repo-root /path/to/repo`. State in the banner that the name comes first, that `status` prints five human lines while `--json` prints the envelope, and that `cgraph index` is how the first index is created.

Keep the file under ~80 lines and keep the comment explaining why the import is lazy.
